== Typed system

= Reduction Trees of $I$-terms 
We will study the identity rewrite system $(Lambda_I, arr_beta)$, and prove that it is equivalent to a rewrite system on trees, allowing
us to use counting argument and general properties on trees.

== Equivalent systems


#let ORTrees = $bold("ORTrees")$
*Tree rewrite system* Define $ORTrees$ the set of all unlabeled rooted ordered (left-to-right) trees of unbounded degree.
We say that $T arr_l T'$ if there is a leaf $u in V_T$ such than $T - u = T'$. We define the $ORTrees$ rewrite system by $(ORTrees, arr_l)$

*Theorem* $(Lambda_I, arr_beta) equiv (ORTrees, arr_l)$

*Proof.* We define inductively the isomorphism $Phi : Lambda_I <--> ORTrees$ :
- Let $M in lambda_I$, write $M = M_1 (M_2 (M_3 ... (M_n I)))$. We then define $Phi(M)$ as the tree with one node and with children $Phi(M_1),..., Phi(M_n)$:
$ Phi(M) := N(Phi(M_1),...,Phi(M_n)) $
- Let $T in ORTrees$ with children $C_1,...,C_n$, we have
$ Phi^(-1) (T) = Phi^(-1) (C_1) space ( Phi^(-1) (C_2) space ... (Phi^(-1) (C_n)  space I)...) $

Then, let's show that it is a ismorphism of structure. Let $C chevron.l I x chevron.r arr_beta C chevron.l x chevron.r$ be a transition. Write $x = x_1 (x_2 (... (x_n I)))$. Then
$ Phi(I x) = N(Phi(I), Phi(x_1),...,Phi(x_n)) $
while
$ Phi(x) = N(Phi(x_1),...,Phi(x_n)) $
so $Phi(I x) arr_l Phi(x)$ by deleting the (leaf) child $Phi(I)$. Since $Phi$ is defined by structural recursion on the applicative structure, it is stable by contexts, hence $Phi(C chevron.l I x chevron.r) arr_l Phi(C chevron.l x chevron.r)$. Conversely, any $arr_l$ step removes a leaf child and corresponds to contracting the associated $I$-redex.

== Counting

For $T in ORTrees$, denote $cN(T)$ the number of way to reduce it into a leaf, so that $cN(T)$ is the number of path from $T$ to the empty tree, counting the multi-edges. Such a path is called a _reduction sequence_ and is represented by a sequence $chevron x_1,...,x_n chevron.r$ of vertices of $T$ such than $x_i$ is a leaf in $T[{x_(i+1),...,x_n}]$. $x_i$ represent morally the $i$-th node to be removed.

*Theorem* For all trees $T$, we have $ N(T) = (|T|!)/(product_(x in V_T) |T(x)|) $


*Proof* By induction on $|T|$. If $|T| = 1$, then there is one way, that is to remove the only node / leaf. Let us consider a tree $T$ with $|T| = n+1$. Denote the root $r$ and the direct children of the root as $x_1,...,x_d$. Let's us fist count the number of path of $T$ in function of $(cN(T(x_i)))_i$:

For all $i<= d$, let $p^((i))$ be a reduction sequence of $A_i$. Remark that for all $i in [d]$ we have $|p^((i))| = |A_i|$. For every partition $X_1 union.sq ... union.sq X_d$ of $[ n ]$ such that $|X_i| = |A_i|$ we can create the reduction sequence $p = chevron p_1,...,p_n chevron.r$ defined by induction by letting $p_(i)$ be the next element in $p^((j))$ such that $i in X_j$ (unique because it is a partition). By appending the root to $p$, we obtain a reduction sequence of $T$.

So for every $d$-uplet of reduction sequence of each children and each partionning of $[n]$ respecting the number of edges of each subtree, we can obtain a reduction sequence. This is a bijective mapping. Let $p$ be a reduction sequence. It ends with $r$, so let's ignore this node. We can extract the subsequence $p^((i))$ from splitting $p_i$ in function of which nodes occurs in which $T(x_i)$. This gives us both the partition and the set of subsequences.

We therefore arrive at this formula :

$ N(A) 
&= binom(n-1,|A_1|,...,|A_k|) product_(i=1)^k F(A_i) 
$

Then for every $i <= d$, $|T(x_i)| <= n$ so we can apply the induction hypothesis to every $T(x_i)$. Denote $n_i = |T(x_i)|$, we have :
$ 
cN(T) 
&= binom(n-1,n_1,...,n_d) product_(i=1)^k F(T(x_i)) \
&= (n-1)!/(n_ 1!...n_k !) product_(i=1)^k (n_i !)/(product_(x in T(x_i)) |T(x_i)|) \
&= (n-1)!/(product_(i=1)^k product_(x in T(x_i)) |T(x_i)|)  \
&= (n-1)!/(product_(x in V_T \\{r}) |T(x_i)|) &union.big.sq_(i<= d) T(x_i) "is a partition of" V_T \\{r}\
&= n!/(product_(x in T) |T(x_i)|) 
$


*Long chain*
#let LC = math.op("LC")
Define inductively the set of long chains of $M in Lambda_I$ as $LC(I) = emptyset$ and for $M != I$, since it can be uniquely written as 
$ M = u_1 space (u_2 space (u_3 space (....(u_d space I)))) $
we define $LC(M) = {(u_1,...,u_d)} union union.big_(i<= d)LC(u_i)$

*Corollary* For $M in lambda_I$ a identity-term, denote $|M|_I$ the number of $I$ in $M$. Then the number of way to reduce $M$ to its normal form $I$ is $ (|M|_I !)/(product_((u_1,...,u_d) in LC(M)) (1+sum_(i<= d) |u_i|_I ) ) $
