== Dyslexic Trees

*Dyslexic Transformation.* For a fixed $P subset.eq NN$, we define a tree transformation $rho_P^i (T)$ with $i$ the height by induction  :
$
rho_P^i(l) &= l " for " l " a leaf" \
rho_P^i (N(x_0,...,x_n))
  &= N(rho_P^(i+1) (x_n),...,rho_P^(i+1) (x_0)) " if " i in P \
rho_P^i (N(x_0,...,x_n))
  &= N(rho_P^(i+1) (x_0),...,rho_P^(i+1) (x_n)) " if " i in.not P
$

*Lemma* $rho_P^i$ is an involution.

*Proof.* By induction on the tree. The leaf case is trivial. For $T = N(x_0,...,x_n)$, if $i in P$, then
$
rho_P^i (T) = N(rho_P^(i+1)(x_n),...,rho_P^(i+1)(x_0)).
$
Applying $rho_P^i$ again reverses the order once more, and by induction each $rho_P^(i+1)(rho_P^(i+1)(x_j)) = x_j$. Hence we get back $N(x_0,...,x_n)$. The case $i in.not P$ is identical but without the reversal of the node itself. Therefore $rho_P^i compose rho_P^i = id$.

*Dyslexic Equivalence* We define the equivalence relation $T scripts(prop)_P T' <==> rho^0_P (T) = T'$ or simply $T prop T'$ if there exists a $P subset.eq NN$ such than $T scripts(equiv)_P T'$. It is an equivalence relation:
- *Symmetry:* If $T scripts(prop)_P T'$, then $rho_P (T') = T$ because $rho_P$ is an involution.
- *Reflexivity:* For all $T$, since  $rho_emptyset (T) = T$ we have $T prop T$.
- *Transitivity:* If $rho^0_(P') (rho^0_(P) (T)) = T'$, then $rho^0_(P xor P') (T) = T'$ with $P xor P'$ the xor (symetric difference) of $P$ and $P'$.

For $T prop T'$, there is an isomorphism as rooted unordered tree $phi_T^P : V_T <--> V_(T')$. We will omit $P$ and $T$ when it's clear from context.
$rho$ is an operation on trees while $phi$ is the operation on the nodes of a fixed tree. We can see that $T equiv T' => rho_P (T) equiv rho_P (T')$ as it is a function defined on rooted ordered trees. 

