*Subtrees* If $S subset.eq V_T$ is connected and contains the root, then $T[S]$ is an induced subtree.
Denote $cal(S)(T)$ the sets of such connected subset containing the root.
Morally, we removes iteratively all the leafs outside of $S$. Notice that $S |-> T[S]$ is not always injective.

*Functoriality Lemma* Let $T in ORTrees$ be an ordered tree and $S in cal(S)(T)$, then $ forall P subset.eq NN, forall i in NN, space rho^i_P (T)[ phi_P^T (S) ] equiv rho_P^i (T[S]). $

*Proof* By induction on $T$. If $T = l$ is a leaf, then necessarily $S = V_T$, hence $rho_P^i (T)[phi_P^T (S)] = l = rho_P^i (T[S]).$
Suppose now $T = N(x_0,...,x_n).$ For every $0 <= i <= n$, let $S_i = S inter V_(x_i).$ Since $S$ is connected and contains the root, every $S_i$ is either empty or belongs to $cal(S)(x_i)$. Assume first that $0 in.not P$. Then $rho_P^0(T) = N( rho_P^1(x_0),..., rho_P^1(x_n))$.

Restricting to the vertices of $phi_P^T (S)$ keeps exactly the children corresponding to non-empty $S_i$. Therefore
$
rho_P^0 (T)[phi_P^T (S)]
=
N( (rho_P^1(x_i)[phi_P^(x_i)(S_i)] )_i ).
$

By induction hypothesis, we have for each $i$ the fact that $rho_P^1(x_i)[phi_P^(x_i)(S_i)] equiv rho_P^1(x_i [S_i]).$ Hence, since the root is in $S$ and not in $P$
$
rho_P^0(T)[phi_P^T (S)] equiv N(( rho_P^1(x_i [S_i]) )_i) equiv rho_P^0 (N((x_i [S_i])_i)) equiv rho_P^0(T[S]).
$

The case $0 in P$ is identical, except that both sides reverse the order of the children simultaneously: we have
$
rho_P^0 (T)[phi_P^T (S)]
=
N( (rho_P^1(x_(n-i))[phi_P^(x_(n-i))(S_i)] )_i ).
$
and therefore
$
rho_P^0(T)[phi_P^T (S)] equiv N(( rho_P^1(x_(n-i) [S_(n-i)]) )_i) equiv rho_P^0 (N((x_i [S_i])_i)) equiv rho_P^0(T[S]).
$

*Corollary* For all $S,S' in cal(S)(T)$ such than $T[S] equiv T[S']$, we have
$ forall P subset.eq NN, space rho^0_P (T)[phi_P^T (S)] equiv rho^0_P (T)[phi_P^T (S')] $
The proof is that since  $T[S] equiv T[S']$, we have $rho^0_P (T[S]) equiv rho^0_P (T[S'])$ so by the functoriality lemma:
$ rho^0_P (T)[phi_P^T (S)] equiv rho_P^0 (T[S]) equiv rho_P^0 (T[S']) equiv rho^0_P (T)[phi_P^T (S')]. $


*Theorem* If $T prop T'$, then $DAG(T) equiv DAG(T')$.

*Proof* Suppose $rho^0_P (T) = T'$. Define the isomorphism :
$
Phi : DAG(T) &<--> &DAG(T') \
T[S] &mapsto.long &rho^0_P (T[S])
$

This is well-defined by the naturality lemma. Since $rho_P^0$ is an involution, the same construction applied from
$T'$ to $T$ gives the inverse map, so we have that $Phi$ is bijective.

Now we show that $Phi$ preserves edges. Since $Phi$ is involutive, we just need to proove that if $A_1 -> A_2$ is an edge in $DAG(T)$ with multiplicity $m$, then $Phi(A_1) -> Phi(1_2)$ is an edge in $DAG(T')$ with multiplicity $m' >= m$.

Suppose that there is an edge $A_1 -> A_2$ in $DAG(T)$ with multiplicity $m$. Then there is $m$ ways to write $A_1 equiv T[S],A_2 equiv T[S']$ where $S' = S - {l}$ for some leaf $l$ of $T[S]$. Denote $M$ the set of couples $(S,l)$ such than $A_1 equiv T[S]$ and $A_2 equiv T[S \\ {l}]$.

Let $(S,l) in M$. Because $phi$ is a rooted tree isomorphism, $phi(l)$ is a leaf of $T'[phi(S)]$. Also, $phi(S \\ {l}) = phi(S) - {phi(l)}$. Therefore there is an edge $T'[phi(S_1)] -> T'[phi(S_2)]$. It remains to check multiplicities. 

Consider the map $gamma :(S,l) |-> (phi(S), phi(l))$. It is injective as $phi$ is. So edge multiplicities are preserved.

Therefore $DAG(T) equiv DAG(T').$

*Corollary* Let $M,N in Lambda_I$. Then if $M prop N$ then $DAG(M) = DAG(N)$.

== Generalizing by a Categorical Approch


Let's define the category $ORTrees$ whose objects are Ordered (left-to-right) Rooted Trees. We'll denote the root as $r$. We define the morphisms $ORTrees(T,T') = {S in cal(S)(T) | T[S] equiv T'}$. We say that a morphism $S in ORTrees(T,T')$ is elementary if $|V_T - S| = 1$ (aka we only remove a single node, a leaf)

*Remarks*
- There is a terminal object, the tree with only a root, with only morphisms $S = {r}$

*Theorem* Let $F : ORTrees --> ORTrees$ be an equivalence of categories. Then
$
  F(T) equiv T' => DAG(T) equiv DAG(T').
$

*Remark* The previous case was just $F(T) = rho_P (T)$ and $F(T ->_S T') = rho_P (T) -->_(phi_P (S)) rho_P (T')$ 

*Proof.* Since $F$ is an equivalence of categories, it is fully faithful and essentially
surjective. In particular, for all trees $A,B$, the map
$
  F_(A,B) : ORTrees(A,B) --> ORTrees(F(A),F(B))
$
is a bijection. We can define $Phi : DAG(T) --> DAG(F(T))$ as $Phi(T') = F(T')$ : it is indeed a application into $"DAG"(T)$ as for all $A in DAG(T)$, we have a morphism $T -->_S A in ORTrees(T,A)$, so a morphism $F(T) -->_(F(S)) F(A) in ORTrees(F(T),F(A))$, so $A in DAG(F(T))$.

Conversely, because $F$ is an equivalence, it has a quasi-inverse $G$. Applying
the same argument to $G$, every vertex of $DAG(T')$ is equivalent to some
$F(A)$ with $A$ a vertex of $DAG(T)$. Hence $Phi$ is a bijection on vertices,
up to $equiv$.

It remains to prove that $Phi$ preserves edges and their multiplicities. This is because a morphism $f : A --> B$ removes exactly one leaf if and only if $f$ is
non-invertible and irreducible (A simple cardinality argument on $S$ in $A -->_S B$ for both ways). Since equivalences of categories preserve and reflect isomorphisms, factorisations,
and irreducibility, every distinct edge of $DAG(T)$ is sent to a distinct edge $F(A) --> F(B)$ of $DAG(T')$. Thus edge multiplicities are preserved.

So $Phi : A |-> F(A)$ induces an isomorphism of directed multigraphs and we have
$
  DAG(T) equiv DAG(T').
$

*Questions!*
- Are all equivalences of categories $F : ORTrees --> ORTrees$ of the form $rho_P$ ?
- If $Psi : ORTrees --> ORTrees$ preserve the DAGs, is it necessary an equivalence of categories? Or can we lighten the hypothesis?

If both questions are yes, we have an iff with $scripts(prop)_P$. If the first is no, we should be able to get a bigger equivalence relation than $scripts(prop)_P$. If the second is false (it probably is), can we get a bigger equivalence using a $Psi$ that isn't an equivalence of categories?

